<p class="gna-lede">Surgery is the main treatment for most cancers of the mouth. It aims to remove the tumour with a margin of healthy tissue, deal with any spread to the lymph nodes in the neck, and rebuild the area so that you can speak, swallow and look as much like yourself as possible. Whether radiotherapy or chemotherapy is added, and what the long-term outlook is, depends mainly on the stage of the cancer — which is why careful planning by a multidisciplinary team comes first.</p>

<h2 id="treatment-intent">Treatment intent: what surgery aims to achieve</h2>
<p>Before any operation, your team should explain the <strong>intent</strong> of treatment. For most people whose cancer is confined to the mouth and neck, surgery is planned with the aim of removing all detectable cancer and controlling the disease in the long term — often called radical or definitive treatment. When cancer is very advanced or has spread to distant organs, the aim may instead be <strong>palliative</strong>: relieving pain, bleeding or difficulty eating, and protecting quality of life.</p>
<p>No honest surgeon can promise an outcome. Results depend on the stage of the cancer, its exact site, how deeply it has grown, whether lymph nodes are involved, what the pathologist finds after surgery, your general health, and whether you stop tobacco and alcohol. Early-stage cancers generally have considerably better outcomes than advanced ones. Our guide to <a href="/blogs/how-long-can-you-live-with-oral-cancer-survival-facts-explained/">oral cancer survival facts</a> explains how outlook is discussed.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Treatment is planned by a multidisciplinary team after biopsy, scans and staging.</li>
    <li>Surgery removes the tumour with a rim of healthy tissue (the margin), which a pathologist then checks.</li>
    <li>Lymph nodes in the neck are often removed at the same operation, even when they feel normal.</li>
    <li>Reconstruction — from simple closure to a free flap of tissue from elsewhere in the body — restores form and function.</li>
    <li>Radiotherapy, sometimes with chemotherapy, is added when the pathology shows a higher risk of recurrence.</li>
    <li>Speech, swallowing and dental rehabilitation are part of treatment, not an afterthought.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="tumour-board">Planning: tests, staging and the tumour board</h2>
<p>Once a biopsy has confirmed cancer — most mouth cancers are squamous cell carcinomas — the next step is to find out exactly how large it is and whether it has spread. This usually involves:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Clinical examination</strong> of the mouth and neck, sometimes under anaesthesia for areas that are hard to see</li>
  <li><strong>CT and/or MRI scans</strong> of the face and neck to show the depth of the tumour, any bone involvement and the lymph nodes</li>
  <li><strong>Chest imaging or a PET-CT scan</strong> for larger cancers, to look for distant spread</li>
  <li><strong>A dental assessment and jaw X-ray</strong>, especially if radiotherapy is likely</li>
  <li><strong>Fitness checks</strong> — blood tests, heart and lung assessment, and a nutrition review</li>
</ul>
<p>These results give the cancer a <strong>stage</strong> using the TNM system: T for the size and depth of the tumour, N for lymph node involvement and M for distant spread. Stages range from I (small and localised) to IV (larger or more widespread).</p>
<p>Good practice is for every new mouth cancer to be discussed at a <strong>multidisciplinary tumour board</strong> before treatment begins. This brings together the head and neck or maxillofacial surgeon, reconstructive surgeon, radiation and medical oncologists, radiologist, pathologist, dental specialist, speech and swallowing therapist, dietitian and specialist nurse. The board agrees a recommended plan, which your surgeon then talks through with you. It is entirely reasonable to ask any team whether your case has been reviewed in this way.</p>

<h2 id="resection-margins">Removing the cancer: resection and margins</h2>
<p>The cancer is removed together with a surrounding cuff of normal-looking tissue — the <strong>margin</strong>. Surgeons usually aim for around a centimetre of healthy tissue around the tumour at the time of surgery, because microscopic cancer cells can extend beyond what can be seen or felt. During the operation, samples from the edges may be sent for rapid "frozen section" examination. Afterwards, the whole specimen is examined in detail, and the pathology report states how close the margins are and whether any cancer cells reach them.</p>
<p>What is removed depends on where the cancer is:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Tongue</strong> — partial glossectomy for smaller cancers, or removal of a larger part of the tongue for advanced ones</li>
  <li><strong>Cheek (buccal mucosa) and gums</strong> — very common sites in India because of tobacco and areca nut chewing; surgery may include part of the jawbone</li>
  <li><strong>Lower jaw (mandible)</strong> — a rim of bone (marginal mandibulectomy) if the cancer is close to it, or a full segment (segmental mandibulectomy) if bone is invaded</li>
  <li><strong>Upper jaw and palate</strong> — partial maxillectomy, sometimes followed by a dental prosthesis called an obturator</li>
  <li><strong>Floor of the mouth and lip</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>Many operations are performed through the mouth, with or without incisions in the neck. Larger resections may need a temporary split of the lip or jaw for access. If you have not yet been diagnosed but have a persistent patch or ulcer, start with our guide to <a href="/blogs/white-patch-in-mouth-leukoplakia/">white patches in the mouth</a>.</p>

<h2 id="neck-dissection">Neck dissection: why the lymph nodes matter</h2>
<p>Mouth cancer most often spreads first to the lymph nodes in the neck. A <strong>neck dissection</strong> removes these nodes and the fatty tissue around them, so the pathologist can check them for cancer.</p>
<ul>
  <li>If nodes are clearly involved, a more extensive dissection is performed.</li>
  <li>If the neck looks normal but the tumour is more than a few millimetres deep, an <strong>elective neck dissection</strong> is usually recommended, because cancer may already be present in nodes too small to see on scans. A large trial from India found that this approach improved survival in early mouth cancer compared with waiting to see whether nodes enlarged.</li>
  <li>For very thin, early cancers, close observation with scans may be an option.</li>
</ul>
<p>Neck dissection leaves a scar, usually placed in a natural skin crease. Possible effects include numbness of the neck skin and earlobe, shoulder stiffness or weakness if the nerve that supports the shoulder is stretched, temporary weakness of the lower lip, swelling under the chin and, rarely, a leak of lymph fluid. Physiotherapy helps shoulder function recover.</p>

