<h2 id="reconstruction">Reconstruction: from simple closure to free flaps</h2>
<p>The goal of reconstruction is to restore the shape of the face and the functions of the mouth — speaking, chewing, swallowing and keeping saliva in. Wherever possible it is done at the same operation as the cancer removal. The options form a ladder:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>What it involves</th><th>Typically used for</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Primary closure</td><td>Stitching the edges together, or allowing a small area to heal</td><td>Small defects of the tongue or cheek</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Skin graft</td><td>A thin layer of skin, usually from the thigh, laid over the area</td><td>Shallow surface defects</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Local or regional flap</td><td>Nearby tissue with its own blood supply rotated into place, such as a nasolabial, submental or chest-wall (pectoralis major) flap</td><td>Moderate defects, or patients less suited to very long surgery</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Soft-tissue free flap</td><td>Skin and fat from the forearm or thigh, with its artery and vein joined to neck vessels under a microscope</td><td>Tongue, floor of mouth and cheek defects</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Bone free flap</td><td>Bone with skin from the lower leg (fibula), hip or shoulder blade, shaped to rebuild the jaw</td><td>Removal of a segment of the lower or upper jaw</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Free flap reconstruction, a form of microvascular surgery, is now standard for many larger defects. For jaw reconstruction, surgeons often use <a href="/blogs/role-of-3d-virtual-planning/">3D virtual surgical planning</a> and cutting guides to shape the bone accurately, and a fibula flap can later support dental implants. The main specific risk is that the joined blood vessels clot, which may need an urgent return to theatre, so flaps are checked closely for the first few days. The donor site heals with a scar, and some people notice temporary weakness or numbness there. Read more about <a href="/skin-grafts-and-flaps">skin grafts and flaps</a> and <a href="/blogs/recovery-protocols-jaw-reconstruction/">recovery after jaw reconstruction</a>.</p>

<h2 id="hospital-recovery">In hospital and the first weeks</h2>
<p>Small operations may need only one or two nights in hospital. After a major resection with free flap reconstruction, a stay of around one to two weeks is common. You may wake with:</p>
<ul>
  <li>A <strong>temporary tracheostomy</strong> — a breathing tube in the front of the neck that protects the airway while swelling settles, usually removed within one to two weeks</li>
  <li>A <strong>feeding tube</strong> through the nose, until it is safe to eat and drink by mouth</li>
  <li><strong>Drains</strong> in the neck, and regular checks of the flap</li>
</ul>
<p>The risks of major head and neck surgery should be discussed openly. They include bleeding, infection, wound breakdown, flap failure, chest infection, blood clots, and changes to speech, swallowing, appearance and sensation. Swelling peaks over the first few days and settles over weeks, and fatigue is common for several weeks after discharge. Recovery varies a great deal between people, and your team should give you a plan specific to your operation.</p>

<h2 id="radiotherapy">Adjuvant radiotherapy and chemotherapy</h2>
<p>When the final pathology report is ready — usually one to two weeks after surgery — the tumour board reviews it. <strong>Adjuvant</strong> (additional) radiotherapy is commonly recommended when there is a higher risk of the cancer returning: larger or deeper tumours, cancer in lymph nodes, close margins, or spread along nerves or small vessels. When cancer cells reach the margin or have broken out of a lymph node, chemotherapy is often given alongside radiotherapy (chemoradiotherapy). Radiotherapy usually starts within about six weeks of surgery and is given on weekdays over roughly six weeks.</p>
<p>Side effects include a sore mouth and throat, skin redness, taste changes, a dry mouth that may be long-lasting, jaw stiffness, and a higher risk of tooth decay and, rarely, jawbone damage (osteoradionecrosis). That is why a dental assessment — and sometimes removal of teeth in poor condition — happens before radiotherapy, and why lifelong fluoride care afterwards matters.</p>

<h2 id="rehabilitation">Speech, swallowing and life after treatment</h2>
<p>Rehabilitation begins in hospital. A <strong>speech and swallowing therapist</strong> teaches exercises to strengthen the tongue and throat and techniques to swallow safely; many people progress from liquids to soft and then normal textures over weeks to months. <strong>Mouth-opening exercises</strong> help prevent stiffness, especially during and after radiotherapy. A <strong>dietitian</strong> supports weight and nutrition, and <strong>dental rehabilitation</strong> — dentures, obturators or implants — restores chewing and appearance. Emotional support matters too; changes to speech and appearance can be hard, and counselling and support groups help many patients and families.</p>
<h3>Follow-up and when to contact your team</h3>
<p>Follow-up visits are frequent in the first two years — often every one to three months — and continue, less often, for at least five years. Stopping tobacco, areca nut and alcohol is one of the most important things you can do, because people treated for mouth cancer have a higher risk of a second cancer. Contact your team promptly about a new ulcer, lump or patch, increasing pain, bleeding, a new swelling in the neck, or difficulty swallowing or breathing. In an emergency, call 108 or go to the nearest emergency department.</p>
